integrated_rules:
Use a symmetric “credible net social-welfare” test: 

1) Identify any *pro-social* pathways the argument claims the AI will produce—requiring credible evidence that the effect is measurable/reliable/predictable in the relevant setting—namely that it:
- measurably improves people’s health or the efficiency of essential real-world services (e.g., earlier detection, better triage, faster assistance), thereby raising living standards;
- meaningfully reduces friction in daily tasks and helps people access services/opportunities they otherwise would struggle to reach;
- helps students and professionals do tasks more effectively/efficiently *without replacing or degrading the capacities those tasks are meant to develop*;
- provides reliable accessibility improvements for marginalized groups, especially people with disabilities;
- improves workplace safety by preventing hazards or reducing accident likelihood/severity.
If one or more of these pro-social conditions are credibly met, the AI is supported as socially beneficial via “better real-world outcomes, access, learning/work productivity, inclusion, and safety.”

2) Identify any *anti-social* pathways the argument claims the AI will produce—also requiring credible evidence that the effect is reliably/predictably present—namely that it:
- reliably generates “answers” that cause people to substitute consuming outputs for independent reasoning/verification (reducing practice in checking evidence, identifying assumptions, or debugging), causing cognitive-capacity harm;
- predictably weakens information trust at individual/civic levels by increasing susceptibility to errors, biased framing, or misinformation;
- predictably harms the economic well-being of many people/businesses (e.g., job displacement or degraded livelihoods) without commensurate offsetting gains;
- repeats or exacerbates discriminatory patterns such that racial minorities are systematically harmed;
- substantially increases collection/inference/sharing of personal data in ways that create significant privacy risk.
If one or more of these anti-social conditions are credibly met, the AI is supported as socially harmful via “cognitive/information degradation, economic/rights harms, discrimination, and privacy risk.”

3) If both pro-social and anti-social conditions are claimed, adjudicate by comparing them *symmetrically* on magnitude, breadth of affected people, likelihood, reversibility (how hard it is to undo harms), and how effective mitigation/guardrails are. The side with the stronger overall balance in those dimensions supports the corresponding conclusion (beneficial vs harmful). If the evidence is insufficient or the comparison comes out roughly even, the verdict remains unresolved rather than defaulting to either conclusion.
